{#- One archive row grammar, shared by the home strip and the archive page, so
    the two cannot drift.

    A quiet day still has a row: it was still reviewed, and a gap in the list
    would say nothing happened rather than nothing was worth publishing. The
    grey bar carries a text label beside it — colour alone never carries the
    meaning.

    The bar is a <span> sized by an inline width percentage. That is the one
    place an inline style is unavoidable: the width is data, not design, and it
    differs per row. Everything else lives in the stylesheet.

    ★ Every row is one line tall (0Z-C). The chips stay on the row, because
    they are what keeps a state from being carried by colour alone; the
    sentence that explained each chip moved to the legend below the list, where
    it is written once instead of once per row.

    ★ Four of them, not six (1F). See the comment in the count column for
    which two came off and what still carries each fact — the states did not
    leave with their chips. -#}
<ul class="uc-rows">
  {% for row in rows %}
  <li class="uc-row{% if row.quiet %} uc-row--quiet{% endif %}{% if row.missing %} uc-row--missing{% endif %}{% if row.backfilled %} uc-row--backfilled{% endif %}{% if row.recent %} uc-row--recent{% endif %}" data-date="{{ row.date }}">
    <span class="uc-row__date">
      {%- if row.missing %}{{ row.date }}
      {%- else %}<a href="{{ issue_href }}{{ row.date }}.html">{{ row.date }}</a>{% endif %}
    </span>
    <span class="uc-row__bar" aria-hidden="true">
      <span class="uc-row__fill" style="width: {{ (row.published / max_items * 100)|round|int }}%"></span>
    </span>
    <span class="uc-row__count">
      {%- if row.missing %}<span class="uc-chip uc-chip--missing">no issue</span>
      {%- elif row.quiet %}<span class="uc-chip">a quiet day</span>
      {%- else %}{{ row.published }} items{% endif %}
      {%- if row.backfilled %} <span class="uc-chip uc-chip--backfilled">filled in later</span>{% endif %}
      {#- ★ Four marks, not six (1F). `no headline` and `no arXiv` came off
          this row because each was the second place saying a fact another
          place already said, and neither state left with its chip:

            `no arXiv`    the issue page's scan line stops claiming the
                          categories arXiv did not read, and says so in words
                          underneath; the operator is mailed (1E, B). `row.silent`
                          is still derived, still on the bar's label and still in
                          the API, and `silent_sources` is still written to the
                          run log.
            `no headline` the issue page writes it where the headline goes
                          — "nothing cleared the headline bar today". `row.unranked`
                          is still derived and still on the bar's label; the
                          lead below is still marked as a title.

          A mark that comes back needs a reason the sentence is not enough, not
          just room on the row. -#}
      {%- if row.recent %} <span class="uc-chip uc-chip--recent">more may follow</span>{% endif %}
    </span>
    {#- ★ The day's headline sentence, not one paper's title (1A, A).

        Three cases, in the order a reader meets them:

        1. The day has a headline. The row shows that sentence — the thing
           written about the day, which is what the reader is scanning for.
        2. The day published papers and none cleared the bar. There is no
           sentence to show, so the row falls back to the representative
           paper's title — and marks it, because an unmarked title in this
           column would read as a headline the day did not have. The
           italic-and-faint styling is what does that marking, and matches how
           a missing day's `reason` is drawn for exactly the same purpose.
           ★ Since 1F it is the only thing on the row that does: the `no
           headline` chip stood beside it and is gone, and YJUN took that
           loss knowingly — the row no longer distinguishes a headline from a
           fallback title in words. The issue page still does.
        3. Neither. Every such day is a backfilled one or predates 0Z-A, which
           is when a sub-threshold day started recording its `work_key` — so
           there is no representative to fall back to. The column stays empty
           rather than promoting `items[0]`, which is a collection order and
           not a judgement about the day.

        Full string in the DOM; the ellipsis is CSS. A string cut in Python is
        cut for a screen reader too — so `title` carries whatever is shown. -#}
    <span class="uc-row__lead" title="{{ row.lead_line or row.lead_title or row.reason }}">
      {%- if row.missing %}<span class="uc-row__reason">{{ row.reason }}</span>
      {%- elif row.lead_line %}{{ row.lead_line }}
      {%- elif row.lead_title %}<span class="uc-row__lead--title">{{ row.lead_title }}</span>
      {%- endif %}
    </span>
    {#- The per-row "1 code, 1 data" column is gone (0Z, Z10). The counts are
        still derived and still reported on the latest issue's stat rail, where
        they describe one day a reader is actually looking at; in a fourteen-row
        table they were a column of ones. -#}
    {#- ★ The one note that survives, and the only one that could (0Z-C).
        Every other note said the same sentence on every row that carried the
        same chip, so it belonged to the chip and moved to the legend. This one
        does not: the reason a live run withheld a day is that day's own, and a
        legend cannot hold "collect.openalex finished FAILED" for one date in
        August. Losing it would undo Y1-2 exactly — a backfilled issue would
        again silently replace the record of the morning it was withheld.
        Rare by construction: it needs a `not_published` run log *and* an issue
        for the same date, which no day in the archive has yet. -#}
    {%- if row.withheld %}
    <span class="uc-row__note">That morning's live run published nothing — {{ row.withheld }}.</span>
    {%- endif %}
  </li>
  {% endfor %}
</ul>
{#- The legend. One sentence per chip, written once under the list instead of
    repeated on every row that carries the chip — which is what made half the
    rows two lines tall.

    Only chips that are actually on this page are explained: a definition for a
    mark the reader cannot see is the same noise in a different place. -#}
{%- set has_backfilled = rows | selectattr("backfilled") | list | length > 0 %}
{%- set has_recent = rows | selectattr("recent") | list | length > 0 %}
{%- set has_missing = rows | selectattr("missing") | list | length > 0 %}
{%- set has_quiet = rows | selectattr("quiet") | list | length > 0 %}
{%- if has_backfilled or has_recent or has_missing or has_quiet %}
<div class="uc-legend">
  <p class="uc-legend__label">What the marks mean</p>
  <dl class="uc-legend__list">
  {%- if has_quiet %}
  <dt><span class="uc-chip">a quiet day</span></dt>
  <dd>The day was reviewed and nothing was worth publishing. It still has a row, because a gap in the list would say nothing happened.</dd>
  {%- endif %}
  {%- if has_missing %}
  <dt><span class="uc-chip uc-chip--missing">no issue</span></dt>
  <dd>The sources did not answer that day, so there is nothing to link to. What went wrong is printed where a title would be.</dd>
  {%- endif %}
  {%- if has_backfilled %}
  <dt><span class="uc-chip uc-chip--backfilled">filled in later</span></dt>
  <dd>Assembled afterwards from the candidates archived for that date, rather than on the morning it covers. It reads a one-day window where a live run reads seven.</dd>
  {%- endif %}
  {%- if has_recent %}
  <dt><span class="uc-chip uc-chip--recent">more may follow</span></dt>
  <dd>arXiv indexes about three days late, so papers posted around those dates can still turn up — in a <em>later</em> issue, never in that one. Issues do not change once published.</dd>
  {%- endif %}
  </dl>
</div>
{%- endif %}
